#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125410} :activity/id #uuid "da1f9dc0-08a1-5819-ae89-7f72ca903238" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050857 :topic/id #uuid "6c2586fe-2403-5a67-b39e-84481a2f1fa7" :topic/math-academy-id 1965} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125410 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125787 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/c1/c1f4086a1ffb63c41af0a9b50c37a0c837cff641fbf1f79add41757b86a15919.svg)\n\nwith the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3.$ Note that this eigenvalue has multiplicity two in the corresponding characteristic equation.\n\nWe know how to find eigenvectors when all eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic equation (i.e., all roots have multiplicity equal to one). But what can we say about the eigenspace $V_{-3}$ for this particular matrix?\n\nRecall that given a matrix with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ is\n\n![](images/dc/dc954c4b48b2ea3bed7a1ab8370d6a2be9a1e22298e9a838acfe47b5b5d0c47d.svg)\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of the matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -11\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 & 16 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}+16{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the system, we obtain the general solution \n\n![](images/a5/a552b978409debb7cf783cd683eceda220ba5eb0f37a82b08add9a66106d75fc.svg)\n\nTherefore, our eigenspace is\n\n![](images/87/8767b25332906b9b6149be3a007cbb89e07d791ed58625e9df369880524f2fdb.svg)\n\nNotice that $s=t=0$ gives the zero vector. On the other hand, the vectors from the general solution form a basis $\\mathcal B$ of our eigenspace:\n\n![](images/06/060bba6a9639bb7658256cea78cf6cb362a3a15a435b3391e806ca43742509ed.svg)\n\nIn particular, we have $\\dim(V_{-3}) =2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5d00ddf6-6fe9-56f2-98f7-dd367ce7b91d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3567 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "aa43a84d-aefd-5022-a90f-64251c19f325" :step/math-academy-id 20103 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125411}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125411 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124524 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124333 :question/id #uuid "a1068af3-0424-51ae-8567-7e1503209d2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d443b8e-3e50-53a7-b3a5-a9d1b85b5f19" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432017}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a One-Dimensional Eigenspace"} :step/id #uuid "c6e8202c-5c82-51bf-86cd-74d8473a657f" :step/math-academy-id 20104 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125412}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125412 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124525 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124334 :question/id #uuid "e82a8038-a2fa-547d-b3ec-32e8f0e4babd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5494"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d945f26-273d-5648-bdee-9092ea61110b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432016}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of a Two-Dimensional Eigenspace"} :step/id #uuid "56638735-6442-509a-9127-6494dc299da9" :step/math-academy-id 20105 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125415}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125788 :tutorial/content "In general, given an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A,$ we can have the following two situations:\n\n- If $\\lambda$ is a simple root of the characteristic equation, then the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ has dimension $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{\\lambda}) = 1\n$$\n\n- If $\\lambda$ is *not* a simple root of the characteristic equation (it's a double, triple, quadruple root, etc.), the dimension of the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ equals the number of free variables in the system of linear equations corresponding to \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The second rule does *not* claim that if an eigenvalue is, say, a double root, then $\\text{dim}(V_{\\lambda}) = 2.$ It says that we have to examine the corresponding linear system and only then make the conclusion depending on the number of free variables.\n\nHowever, there is one thing we can determine about the dimension of the eigenspace, and it is given by the following theorem.\n\n*If an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is a root of multiplicity $k$ in the corresponding characteristic equation, then the eigenspace $V_{\\lambda}$ has dimension not greater than $k \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(V_{\\lambda}) \\leq k\n$$\n\n*\n\nRecall that the multiplicity of an eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic equation is called *algebraic multiplicity*. On the other hand, the dimension of the corresponding eigenspace is called the **geometric multiplicity** of the eigenvalue. Our theorem above states the following: \n\n*The geometric multiplicity of an eigenvalue is always less than or equal to the algebraic multiplicity.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "37e113e4-2071-5b13-ad84-c723f4af6af3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3568 :tutorial/title "Dimensions of Eigenspaces"} :step/id #uuid "93c2d51a-9d9a-5ac0-818c-a24eb575a3af" :step/math-academy-id 20106 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125414}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125414 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124526 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124335 :question/id #uuid "7f15ec22-8a8e-5547-9aa1-7ffa8b1555a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5495"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0631858c-a25d-564a-8b0f-b21506d844a2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050376}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432020}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dimension of an Eigenspace"} :step/id #uuid "5f686067-1777-558d-a629-9db39fffcbca" :step/math-academy-id 20107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125415 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124527 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124336 :question/id #uuid "483a0b64-adfa-50b7-a738-2cdc135b1163" :question/math-academy-id "e-5496"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e7473be-4d68-5d31-817f-adb7cf288e97" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432019}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "32c7f37c-04ef-5f79-bbe2-b2b9d0086964" :step/math-academy-id 20108 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125413}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix in the General Case" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124750}]}
